<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!--
      DS-022: the app's one theme, declared on <application> in AndroidManifest.xml so that every
      activity resolves it rather than each one having to repeat it. It exists to take the
      platform's action bar away: with no theme declared anywhere, the platform supplies its own,
      which draws a bar titled from android:label above whatever the screen composes — two headers
      on screen where DS-021 allows exactly one, and the one the design system owns is the one the
      user cannot see (#132).

      The parent is a platform theme because :app depends on neither AppCompat nor the Material
      Components library: ADR 0007 keeps Material out of :app, and the Compose Material 3 that
      :designsystem uses is a Compose library with no XML themes to inherit from. Theme.Material is
      the dark variant rather than .Light because DesignSystemColors.Default is unconditionally
      dark (DS-050 — bg is #121212) and there is no light palette for a DayNight parent to switch
      to.

      windowActionBar and windowNoTitle are written out here rather than left to the parent's name,
      per design-system.md's fifth invariant: the parent stays an implementation choice, the
      absence of the action bar does not, and a later parent change cannot quietly put the second
      header back.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.IntervalTrainer" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
    </style>

</resources>
